	url = {https://doi.org/10.1209/0295-5075/3/3/011},
	doi = {10.1209/0295-5075/3/3/011},
	abstract = {We estimate the persistence length L over which a charged polymer is a rigid rod, as influenced by other polymers. Using energy balance arguments, we find that L is much smaller than the electrostatic persistence length Le. Without added salt, L is of the order of the distance Λ between chains. With added salt, L is a heretofore unrecognized length scale: Λ ≪ L ≪ Le. From this L we postulate the dependence of viscosity on polymer concentration, ionic strength and molecular weight. The concentration dependence accounts for hitherto unexplained empirical laws.},
